# OpenCode host map

Apply the shared rules above using the tools exposed in this session.

| Written | OpenCode |
|---|---|
| load the `<name>` skill, `Skill` | `skill` with `name`. Resolve references inside `~/.agents/skills/<name>/` |
| `Read`, `Edit`, `Write`, `Bash`, `LSP` | `read`, `edit`, `apply_patch`, `bash`, `lsp`, or the equivalent tool listed here |
| spawn, dispatch, `Agent` | `task` with `subagent_type` set to the role name |
| message or resume an agent, `SendMessage` | Call `task` with the returned `task_id` and the same `subagent_type` |
| list agents, `ListAgents` | The roles advertised by `task`. There is no separate roster or slot-count tool |
| a structured question, `AskUserQuestion` | `question` with `questions`. Use at most four questions per call |
| parallel dispatch | Independent `task` calls in one response. Wait for every report before advancing the batch. Use background mode only when it is exposed |
| background process, `run_in_background`, `TaskStop` | The shell's exposed process/session controls. If absent, use a bounded command. Never assume a process-control tool exists |
| the browser tool | `playwright_*`, enabled only for `tester`, `uxui-designer`, and `uxui-design-review` |
| the Expo MCP tools | `Expo_*` |
| permission rules and settings | `permission` in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` and agent frontmatter |
| an agent definition, model and effort | `~/.config/opencode/agents/*.md`. `variant` selects effort |
| the project's MCP config | `mcp` in `opencode.json` or `.opencode/opencode.json` |
| `/tmp/agent/`, scratchpad | `/tmp/agent/`. An explicit session temp directory takes precedence |
| live agent config | `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`, `~/.config/opencode/agents/`, and `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` |

## Delegation

- Agent bodies come from Claude's agent files through the Codex templates. Interpret their tool names using this map.
- `general` and `explore` are disabled. Primary agents are not delegation targets.
- Only planner and coordinator can delegate. Planner spawns scouts. Coordinator spawns the execution skill's ten leaf roles. Neither can spawn a planner or coordinator. `subagent_depth: 2` permits lead → planner/coordinator → leaf.
- Planner loads `implementation-plan-creator`. Coordinator loads `implement-plan-execution`. Claude's `skills:` preload does not carry into OpenCode role files.
- The managed task-authorization plugin rechecks caller and session permissions before each task. Task prompts use plain file paths or backtick-quoted literal mentions. Unquoted `@` references are rejected because the pinned CLI expands them before native authorization. Resumes must belong to the same parent and role.
- A foreground `task` call returns the report when the subagent's turn ends. Never resume the same session concurrently.
- Background tasks require `OPENCODE_EXPERIMENTAL_BACKGROUND_SUBAGENTS=true` at startup. If `background` is absent from the tool schema, use foreground calls. Collect every report before verification, acceptance, or close-out.
- `plan` and `advisor` are read-only session presets. They can delegate only to `scout`, `reviewer`, and `security-reviewer`.
- Scout, reviewers, tester, and uxui-design-review cannot use file-edit tools. OpenCode has no filesystem sandbox. Their shell commands must honor the same read-only bounds.
- All three reviewers deny web and MCP tools. Their verification commands must remain local.

## Permissions and machine state

- Routine tools, shell commands, and managed MCP tools are allowed by default. OpenCode has no automatic safety reviewer.
- Never enable `--auto` to override the configured rules. Repeated identical tool calls are denied by `doom_loop`.
- File-read denies do not constrain shell commands, content searches, language servers, or MCP tools. Inspect their targets and never use another tool to read a denied file.
- Project config and agent rules can override global permissions.
- Check each MCP tool's target and effect before a write. An allowed tool can still perform a destructive action.
- Authentication, sessions, caches, generated dependency files, and interactive preferences stay unmanaged. Never commit this machine state.
